## Why This Is Asked
Tests endianness understanding and safe 128-bit lane manipulation with pure bitwise ops.

## Key Concepts
- Byte reversal with masks
- 128-bit struct arithmetic
- Endianness swapping

## Code Example
```javascript
static inline uint64_t reverseBytes64(uint64_t x){
  x = ((x & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL) << 8) | ((x >> 8) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL);
  x = ((x & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL) << 16) | ((x >> 16) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL);
  x = (x << 32) | (x >> 32);
  return x;
}

uint128 swapEndian128(uint128 x){
  uint128 r;
  r.hi = reverseBytes64(x.lo);
  r.lo = reverseBytes64(x.hi);
  return r;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for a compile-time constant shift? 
- How to verify with unit tests across edge cases (all zeros, all ones, cross-boundary values)?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to implement bit-twiddling without loops, using SWAR patterns for constant-time population count.

## Key Concepts
- SWAR (Small/Word-level Parallelism)
- 64-bit masks and carry-free sums
- No intrinsics or branches; deterministic performance

## Code Example
```javascript
uint64_t popcount64(uint64_t x) {
  x = x - ((x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  x = (x + (x >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  return (x * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this to count bits in 128-bit values? 
- What are the trade-offs vs. hardware popcnt instructions in modern CPUs?

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes fluency with SWAR bit-twiddling and 128-bit composition, ensuring branchless correctness.

## Key Concepts
- 64-bit SWAR reversal
- 128-bit result from {hi: rev64(lo), lo: rev64(hi)}
- Mask/shift sequences to flip bit blocks without branches

## Code Example
```c
static inline uint64_t rev64(uint64_t x){
  x = (x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL | (x & 0x5555555555555555ULL) << 1;
  x = (x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL | (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) << 2;
  x = (x >> 4) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL | (x & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL) << 4;
  x = (x >> 8) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL | (x & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL) << 8;
  x = (x >> 16) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL | (x & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL) << 16;
  x = (x >> 32) | (x << 32);
  return x;
}

uint128 reverseBits128(uint128 x){
  uint128 r;
  r.hi = rev64(x.lo);
  r.lo = rev64(x.hi);
  return r;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test edge alignment and performance implications?
- Could this approach be generalized to arbitrary widths with minimal changes?

## Why This Is Asked

Parity computation over 128 bits tests the ability to design constant-time reductions using only bitwise operations and shifts, without loops or branches.

## Key Concepts
- Bitwise XOR folding across 128 bits
- Progressive reduction by shifting (64,32,16,8,4,2,1)
- Final extraction with a single bit AND

## Code Example
```javascript
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128_t;
unsigned parity128(uint128_t x) {
  uint64_t v = x.hi ^ x.lo;
  v ^= v >> 32;
  v ^= v >> 16;
  v ^= v >> 8;
  v ^= v >> 4;
  v ^= v >> 2;
  v ^= v >> 1;
  return v & 1;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend this to 256 bits while preserving the same approach?
- Can you prove the operation is constant time regardless of input bit distribution?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests mastery of low-level bit hacks: packing, masking, and fixed-time transformations without control flow.

## Key Concepts
- Morton (Z-order) interleaving
- Bit spreading via staged masks
- Fixed, branchless implementation

## Code Example
```cpp
uint32_t spread16(uint16_t v){
  uint32_t x = v;
  x = (x | (x << 8)) & 0x00FF00FF;
  x = (x | (x << 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F;
  x = (x | (x << 2)) & 0x33333333;
  x = (x | (x << 1)) & 0x55555555;
  return x;
}
uint32_t interleave16(uint16_t a, uint16_t b){
  return spread16(a) | (spread16(b) << 1);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you generalize to interleave 32-bit values into a 64-bit result?
- What tests would you add to validate edge cases like a or b == 0xFFFF?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests knowledge of per-byte bit-twiddling, branchless design, and parallel bit tricks that map well to performance-critical code paths in systems programming.

## Key Concepts
- Byte-wide parity, parallel bit tricks, constant-time operations
- Masking to extract per-byte indicators
- No branches or loops

## Code Example
```c
uint64_t perByteParity64(uint64_t x) {
  uint64_t y = x;
  y ^= y >> 1;
  y ^= y >> 2;
  y ^= y >> 4;
  return y & 0x0101010101010101ULL;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
