## Why This Is Asked
Assesses non-branching bit extraction (compression-like utility) crucial in low-latency paths. It tests SWAR techniques, mask reasoning, and edge-case handling for arbitrary masks.\n\n## Key Concepts
- SWAR bit-twiddling for packing bits\n- Mask-driven selection and order preservation\n- Handling 64-bit boundaries without loops\n\n## Code Example
```c
uint64_t compressBits64(uint64_t x, uint64_t mask) {
  x &= mask;
  x = (x ^ (x >> 1)) & 0x5555555555555555ULL;
  x = (x ^ (x >> 2)) & 0x3333333333333333ULL;
  x = (x ^ (x >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  x = (x ^ (x >> 8)) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL;
  x = (x ^ (x >> 16)) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL;
  x = (x ^ (x >> 32)) & 0x00000000FFFFFFFFULL;
  return x;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to 128-bit inputs?\n- What are the performance implications on modern CPUs with this fixed-sequence approach?

## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates the ability to unpack bit-packed fields without branches—a common low-level task in networking, codecs, and embedded systems.

## Key Concepts

- **Bitwise masks and shifts** extract subfields from packed integers efficiently
- **No branches** ensures constant-time execution, critical for performance-sensitive code
- **Endianness** is irrelevant when masking at exact bit positions

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct {
    uint8_t  version;
    uint8_t  type;
    uint16_t length;
    uint8_t  flags;
} Header;

Header extractHeaderFields(uint32_t w) {
    Header h;
    h.version = (w >> 28) & 0xF;  // Bits 31-28
    h.type    = (w >> 24) & 0xF;  // Bits 27-24
    h.length  = (w >> 8)  & 0xFFFF; // Bits 23-8
    h.flags   = w & 0xFF;          // Bits 7-0
    return h;
}
```

Each shift moves the target field to the least significant position, then the mask isolates the relevant bits. This approach is branchless, deterministic, and executes in constant time.

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to reason with 128-bit data layout and perform blockwise rearrangements using only bitwise ops. It also checks handling of multi-word operands without branching.

## Key Concepts

- Block masking and extraction
- Cross-word recombination
- Branchless 128-bit manipulation

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

uint128 swapAdjacentBlocks128(uint128 x, unsigned block) {
    uint64_t M = (1ULL << block) - 1;
    uint64_t A0 = x.lo & M;
    uint64_t A1 = (x.lo >> block) & M;
    uint64_t A2 = x.hi & M;
    uint64_t A3 = (x.hi >> block) & M;
    uint64_t loP = (A0 << block) | A1;
    uint64_t hiP = (A2 << block) | A3;
    return (uint128){.hi = hiP, .lo = loP};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend to non-uniform block counts or dynamic block that isn't divisor of 128?
- How to optimize for block=16 across many values in tight loops?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests a branchless, efficient 128-bit bit-count using a proven 64-bit SWAR approach on two halves.

## Key Concepts
- SWAR (SIMD Within A Register) popcount on 64-bit chunks
- Treat 128-bit as two independent 64-bit parts
- Branchless, constant-time operations

## Code Example
```javascript
// C-like helper (conceptual)
static inline uint8_t pop64(uint64_t v){
  v -= (v>>1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL;
  v = (v & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((v>>2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  v = (v + (v>>4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  return (v * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;
}
uint8_t countSetBits128(uint128 x){
  return pop64(x.hi) + pop64(x.lo);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests branchless 128-bit rotation using only shifts.

## Key Concepts
- View 128-bit value via 128-bit type
- Branchless rotation with shifts and modulo
- Correct unpacking into hi/lo

## Code Example
```c
void rotateRight128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo, unsigned int k, uint64_t *out_hi, uint64_t *out_lo) {
  unsigned int kk = k & 127;
  __uint128_t v = (((__uint128_t)hi) << 64) | lo;
  v = (v >> kk) | (v << ((128 - kk) % 128));
  *out_hi = (uint64_t)(v >> 64);
  *out_lo = (uint64_t)v;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you implement without 128-bit type support?
- What are performance trade-offs for different compilers?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests advanced 128-bit bit-twiddling, boundary shifts, and correctness under rotation.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit values represented as hi/lo 64-bit halves
- Rotation across a 64-bit boundary
- Handling n in [0,127] with minimal branching in production

## Code Example
```javascript
uint128 rotateLeft128(uint128 x, unsigned n){
  n &= 127; // modulo 128
  if (n < 64){
    uint64_t hi = (x.hi << n) | (x.lo >> (64 - n));
    uint64_t lo = (x.lo << n) | (x.hi >> (64 - n));
    return (uint128){.hi = hi, .lo = lo};
  } else {
    unsigned k = n - 64;
    uint64_t hi = (x.lo << k) | (x.hi >> (64 - k));
    uint64_t lo = (x.hi << k) | (x.lo >> (64 - k));
    return (uint128){.hi = hi, .lo = lo};
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- Can you make it fully branchless with masks?
- How would you adapt for different word sizes or endianness?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of bit-level folding to compute parity with no branches.

## Key Concepts
- Parity calculation
- Bitwise shifts and XORs
- Constant-time operations

## Code Example
```javascript
uint8_t parity32(uint32_t x){
  x ^= x >> 16;
  x ^= x >> 8;
  x ^= x >> 4;
  x ^= x >> 2;
  x ^= x >> 1;
  return x & 1;
}
```

